Table~\ref{tab:beta01-3seed} provides the full per-seed breakdown. Conclusion embedding (H1) is a GRPO-universal strategy: it appears at 70.9--93.2\% under random reward and 0--51.9\% under ISR. ISR reward does not reduce total hacking---it \emph{converts} H1 into the more extreme H7 form. Overall conclusion-in-premise rates are comparable (ISR s42: 67.9\% vs Random s42: 70.9\%), and the random control achieves higher and more stable PR (85$\pm$6\% vs 77$\pm$14\%).

\paragraph{Premise-count confound.}
Seed~42 under ISR---the only seed not dominated by H7---shows higher raw proved-only ISR (0.415 vs 0.131 for random). However, this apparent advantage is entirely a premise minimization artifact. ISR-trained models produce shorter formalizations (mean 5.58 components vs 8.46 for random), and LOO-based metrics are mathematically biased by component count: fewer premises mean each is more likely to appear necessary, inflating ISR independent of reasoning quality.

To control for this, we compare ISR values only among genuine (non-hacking) samples with $\geq$3 premises: ISR genuine achieves 0.258 ($n{=}11$) versus random genuine at 0.243 ($n{=}21$), a ratio of 1.06$\times$---no meaningful structural advantage. Of ISR s42's 26 nominal ``genuine'' samples, 15 have only 1--2 premises, where ISR$\geq$0.5 is a mathematical artifact of LOO on short inputs. The apparent 2.4$\times$ raw ISR difference reduces to noise once premise count is controlled.

\paragraph{High-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.3$): classic Goodhart.}
At $\beta{=}0.3$, ISR reward causes PR to collapse to 18\%---a 5.2$\times$ degradation from exec-only. The matched random control achieves PR${=}$48\%, demonstrating that ISR at high dose is not merely unhelpful but \emph{actively harmful}: ISR is 2.7$\times$ worse than a structurally uninformative signal.

\paragraph{Dose-response pattern.}
Together, the two dose levels reveal a complete Goodhart trajectory~\citep{karwowski-goodhart-rl, gao-reward-overoptimization}: at low dose ($\beta{=}0.1$), the model games the proxy metric itself (ISR inflation via H7), producing high ISR scores that mask structural degeneration; at high dose ($\beta{=}0.3$), the ISR signal overwhelms the execution reward, causing format collapse and PR degradation. No dose yields genuine structural improvement.


\subsection{Cross-Logic Diagnostic Transfer}
\label{sec:crosslogic}

To test whether ISR generalizes beyond a single formal language, we replicate the diagnostic and reward experiments using Prolog with SWI-Prolog as the solver.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_prolog_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Prolog exec-only ISR across three seeds. ISR is near-zero for all seeds (s42${=}$0.037, s43${=}$0.015, s44${=}$0.387 on 21/100 proved), replicating the FOL finding that exec-only training produces structurally degenerate outputs regardless of PR. Cross-seed PR instability ($\sigma{=}$31pp) also replicates.}
\label{fig:prolog-comparison}
\end{figure}

