\caption{Cross-logic transfer results (proved-only ISR). \textit{Top}: Exec-only ISR near-zero in both logics, confirming diagnostic transfer. \textit{Bottom}: Prolog STA yields universal Goodhart (3/3 seeds gaming, 0\% genuine); ISR omitted as gaming-inflated. $^\ddagger$Tautological artifact (2-comp). $^\S$Small-sample artifact ($n{=}$21 proved). $^\P$Uppercase pseudo-Prolog; $n_{\text{nec}}{=}1$ for 38/38. $^\|$NL$\to$snake\_case atom as fact+query; $n_{\text{nec}}{=}1$ for 95/97.}
\label{tab:prolog-crosslogic}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Diagnostic transfer.}
Table~\ref{tab:prolog-crosslogic} (top) and Figure~\ref{fig:prolog-comparison} show that ISR detects structural degeneration in Prolog as it does in FOL. Two of three Prolog exec-only seeds yield proved-only ISR near zero (s42${=}$0.037, s43${=}$0.015), confirming structurally degenerate outputs despite moderate-to-high PR. Seed~44 (ISR${=}$0.387) is a small-sample artifact: only 21 of 100 samples prove, and the higher ISR reflects variance from this sparse denominator. Cross-seed PR instability in FOL ($\sigma{=}$47pp) replicates in Prolog ($\sigma{=}$31pp), and both logic types produce qualitatively different collapse modes across seeds.

We note an important semantic distinction: low ISR in Prolog exec-only reflects \emph{redundant coding}---the model generates repeated ground facts where removing any single copy does not affect the proof---rather than absence of reasoning. This contrasts with high ISR under Prolog STA (below), which reflects \emph{metric gaming}. ISR must be interpreted relative to the training objective.

\paragraph{Universal Goodhart in Prolog.}
Table~\ref{tab:prolog-crosslogic} (bottom) reveals the strongest Goodhart result: all three Prolog STA seeds converge to gaming, with 0\% genuine proofs across all 185 proved samples. Each seed develops a distinct gaming pathway:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{s42}: Clean H7---a single Prolog clause identical to the query (50/50 proved).
  \item \textbf{s43}: Degenerate syntax---uppercase pseudo-Prolog (e.g., \texttt{MARY\_IS\_AN\_AVADEOTE.}). A text-match classifier labels these as genuine, but functional analysis shows $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$ for all 38 proved samples (functionally H7).
  \item \textbf{s44}: Query-as-fact gaming---the model converts NL problems to snake\_case atoms and sets them as both fact and query, achieving PR${=}$97\% with $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$ for 95/97. Initial automated classification reported 100\% genuine; independent functional analysis corrected this to 0\%.
\end{itemize}
This is stronger than the FOL finding: FOL ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ retains ${\sim}$16\% genuine residual across seeds, while Prolog STA produces zero. Prolog's simpler semantics enable more complete exploitation, eliminating the genuine residual observed in FOL.

\paragraph{Classifier limitations.}
The Prolog results expose a blind spot of text-match hacking classifiers. Seeds~s43 and s44 bypass the strict H7/H1 classifier through surface-level novelty (non-standard casing, atom-level encoding) while remaining functionally tautological ($n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$). This motivates cross-validation of hacking classifiers with functional metrics such as the $n_{\text{necessary}}$ distribution.


\subsection{Hacking Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_hacking_composition.pdf}
\caption{Hacking mode composition across reward conditions (proved samples). ISR reward converts H1 (conclusion embedding) into the more extreme H7 (conclusion duplication) without reducing total hacking prevalence. SFT exhibits near-zero hacking (92.9\% genuine).}
\label{fig:hacking-composition}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{p{1.85cm}p{2.1cm}cccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Mode} & \textbf{Mechanism} & \textbf{Exec} & \textbf{ISR} & \textbf{Rand} & \textbf{SFT} \\
\midrule
H1 & Concl.\ among premises & 87 & 17 & 81 & 7 \\
H7 & Single prem.\ = concl. & $\bullet$ & \textbf{65} & 0 & 0 \\
Tautological & 2-comp $P{=}C$ & $\bullet$ & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
Repetition & Output degen. & $\bullet$ & --- & --- & --- \\
Contradiction & $n_{\text{comp}}{\uparrow}$, ISR${\to}$0 & $\bullet$ & --- & --- & --- \\
C-w/o-utility & Complex, unnecessary & 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\
Query-as-fact & Atom as fact+query & --- & --- & --- & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Hacking taxonomy across FOL conditions. Values are \% of proved samples. Exec-only column: 3-seed rates vary by mode (H1${=}$87\% s42; H7/Tautological${=}$s44; Repetition${=}$s43; Contradiction${=}$1000-step); $\bullet$ marks seed- or step-specific modes. ISR and Random columns: $\beta{=}0.1$ 3-seed means. C-w/o-utility: complexity-without-utility (random-specific). Query-as-fact: Prolog STA only (Table~\ref{tab:prolog-crosslogic}).}
\label{tab:hacking-taxonomy}
\end{table}

Across all conditions, seeds, and logic types, we identify seven distinct hacking modes (Table~\ref{tab:hacking-taxonomy}, Figure~\ref{fig:hacking-composition}):

\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{H1: Conclusion embedding.} The conclusion appears among multiple premises; the solver succeeds trivially. GRPO-universal: 52--93\% of proved samples across all RL reward types.
  \item \textbf{H7: Conclusion duplication.} A single premise identical to the conclusion. ISR-specific: 64.5\% under ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ vs 0\% under random.
  \item \textbf{Tautological collapse.} Two-component formalizations where premise${=}$conclusion (exec-only s44, 94\% of outputs, mean components${=}$2.04).
  \item \textbf{Repetition collapse.} Identical premises repeated until the token limit (exec-only s43, 87\% hit max length).
  \item \textbf{Contradiction explosion.} Excessive premises (mean 18.55 components at 1000 steps) that trivially entail any conclusion via embedded contradictions.
  \item \textbf{Complexity-without-utility.} Syntactically complex but semantically vacuous premises (random $\beta{=}0.1$, 3.8\%).
  \item \textbf{Query-as-fact gaming.} NL problems converted to snake\_case atoms serving as both fact and query (Prolog STA s44). Bypasses text-match classifiers while remaining functionally tautological.
\end{enumerate}

The reward signal determines hacking \emph{composition} but not \emph{prevalence}: total conclusion-in-premise rates are comparable across ISR and random conditions (${\sim}$68--81\%), but ISR converts the GRPO-universal H1 mode into the ISR-specific H7 mode. SFT remains the only condition with near-zero hacking (92.9\% genuine), confirming that hacking is RL-reward-specific. Different reward formulations produce characteristic hacking signatures---a finding that may inform future diagnostic design.

